---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { siteConfig } from '../config/site'
import { getPrograms } from '../lib/content'

const programs = await getPrograms()
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="How Story Sprout started, how it is built, and the role the AI illustrator plays."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Children are the authors.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Story Sprout is an educational program, a creative platform, and a research project in
        which children write their own stories and turn them into picture books.
      </p>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>How it started</h2>
        <p>
          In summer 2024 our team helped make AI-generated videos for a children's music
          program at {siteConfig.parentOrg.name}. That autumn we began writing weekly science
          lessons and leading volunteers at an after-school program. After a year, the center
          asked us to create a program for its 2025 summer camp — and let us choose the goal.
        </p>
        <p>
          We wanted to improve children's literacy. So we inverted the idea we had started
          with: instead of adults using AI to make media for children, children would write
          their own stories and direct AI to make the pictures. That became Story Sprout.
        </p>

        <h2>Why we built our own platform</h2>
        <p>
          We designed the curriculum and ran the first workshops alongside the center's
          volunteers. The children were far more engaged than we expected. But the free
          commercial image site we were using was not made for them: students tested its
          limits, and it sometimes displayed inappropriate ads. Once, one of us instinctively
          covered an ad with a hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          So Story Sprout needed a platform of its own. With {siteConfig.parentOrg.name}'s
          support we formed a three-student team, designed the interface and its key features,
          and led ten months of development from August 2025 to June 2026 — including
          local-only storage and separate teacher and student modes. We tested the beta in
          spring 2026.
        </p>

        <h2>Why the curriculum works</h2>
        <p>
          The children's engagement made us curious about <em>why</em> it was working. We
          cold-emailed 25 professors in the learning sciences and child–computer interaction.
          Three replied with detailed advice and papers to read. After about seventeen papers
          and many conversations, we came to see the curriculum as a cycle: a child expresses
          an idea, sees it rendered, judges it, and revises it.
        </p>
        <p>
          We called that the <strong>Nested Cognitive Mirror</strong>. The framework gave the
          platform a clearer direction — children remain the authors and the judges, and the
          AI illustrates. We built those decisions in, tested them across the 2026 summer
          sessions, and kept improving the platform as we used it. The work is written up on
          our <a href="/research/">research page</a>, alongside the studio's source code.
        </p>

        <h2>What children actually do</h2>
        <p>
          The child supplies the story language and decides what belongs in the book. When it
          is time to illustrate a page, the child describes the scene they have in mind. The AI
          illustrator renders an image from that description. The child then looks at what came
          back, decides whether it matches the story they meant to tell, and revises the
          description until it does.
        </p>
        <p>
          That loop — describe, look closely, say it more clearly — is the point. The AI is
          used as an illustrator, not as the author of the child's story.
        </p>

        <h2>The studio</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={siteConfig.platformUrl} rel="noopener" data-cta="studio" data-cta-location="about">Story Sprout Studio</a> runs in the
          browser. A project and its revision history stay in a local file the supervising
          adult controls; there are no child accounts. Teacher and student modes are separate.
          The whole thing is <a href={siteConfig.codeUrl} rel="noopener">open source</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Who it is for</h2>
        <p>
          The current target age range is approximately 7–13, with some activities designed
          especially for younger children. The curriculum is free to download and use, and runs
          as 4 to 8 sessions.
        </p>

        <h2>Where it runs</h2>
        <p>
          We continue to run workshops at the original center, and we now train educators and
          volunteers through <a href="/#training">webinars</a> so they can run Story Sprout in
          their own classroom or community group.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {
            programs.map((program) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/programs/${program.id}/`}>{program.data.title}</a>
                {program.data.formatDisplay && (
                  <span class="muted"> — {program.data.formatDisplay}</span>
                )}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        <h2>Who runs it</h2>
        <p>
          Story Sprout is a program of{' '}
          <a href={siteConfig.parentOrg.url} rel="noopener">{siteConfig.parentOrg.name}</a>, a{' '}
          {siteConfig.parentOrg.taxStatusDisplay}. To keep the platform free for educators, we
          apply for grants and seek donations — you can{' '}
          <a href="/donate/">support the program here</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
